Final Questions:

1. Does insurance cost increase with age?
Yes, it definitely does. Both the scatter plot and the positive slope (about 240) show that older people pay more for insurance. On average, each extra year of age adds about 240 dollars to the cost.

2. Which features might influence insurance charges?
Besides age, whether a person smokes or not has a huge effect on the price. Other things like their BMI (weight/health), the number of children they have, and their region also influence the cost.

3. How close are the predicted values to the actual values?
They follow the general upward line, but they are not very close for individual people. The MAE is around 9,135 dollars, so the predictions are off by a fair amount. This happens because the model only looks at age and has no idea whether someone is a smoker or has a high BMI.

4. Is your model performing reasonably well? Explain briefly.
It gives a good basic baseline to see the age trend, but an R2 of 0.12 means age only explains about 12% of the variation in price. It needs other features like smoker status and BMI to become really accurate.

5. Where can this model be used in real life?
An insurance company can use it as a simple baseline tool to give quick initial price estimates based on age, and customers can use it to get an idea of how their insurance expenses will go up as they get older.
